#!/usr/bin/env bash
#MISE description="PreToolUse hook body: deny a handoff to a human (ExitPlanMode, AskUserQuestion) while no background hold is live"
#
# CLOUD-451. Wired from `.claude/settings.json`. Thin by design — the decision
# lives in `plan-hold-check`, where it is unit-tested by
# `tests/plan-hold-guard.bats` and the sentinel path is spelled once.
#
# **Why this is a deny and not a nudge.** CLOUD-97 and CLOUD-219 each ruled out
# hard denies at the Stop boundary, because pausing mid-task is legitimate and
# blocking "done" punishes correct behaviour. This is a different boundary: the
# blocked call is the handoff ITSELF, nothing is mid-flight, and the remedy is
# one tool call away. An advisory here would also arrive too late to help — the
# turn it warns about is the turn already ending.
#
# **Why this is a second `PreToolUse` entry rather than a `batten.toml` row.**
# CLOUD-312 made the engine the one pre-tool entry point, and a rule there would
# be the right home — except that the predicate is "a named background process is
# live", which no rule kind expresses. `Shape` matches a command line, `Receipt`
# reads a SHA-keyed verification claim; neither can see a pid. That is a linked
# capability gap on CLOUD-451 §8, and until it closes the predicate has to run as
# its own entry.
#
# **Its cost is not the cost CLOUD-435 removed.** That deletion's argument was
# per-TOOL-CALL: six `mise run` launches on every Bash call, ~150 ms each to do
# ~11 ms of policy. Its own criterion is what licenses this row — `SessionStart`,
# `UserPromptSubmit` and `Stop` were kept because they "fire once per
# session/prompt/turn, not per tool call", and `ExitPlanMode`/`AskUserQuestion`
# are in exactly that class: at most once per turn, and only on turns about to
# stop and wait for a person. The matcher is those two tools and nothing else, so
# no Bash call pays for it, and the wiring invokes this file BY PATH — the same
# reason `batten-hook.sh` execs a binary instead of going through the task runner.
#
# Fails OPEN on anything it cannot parse, and on BATTEN_PLAN_HOLD_BYPASS=1 — for
# the genuine case of deliberately ending a turn idle. Fail-open matters more
# here than usual: this guard sits on the path a session takes to ask a human
# anything, so a guard that jammed would make the session unable to ask for help
# with the fact that it cannot ask for help.
set -uo pipefail

[ -n "${BATTEN_PLAN_HOLD_BYPASS:-}" ] && exit 0

raw=$(cat) || exit 0

tool=$(printf '%s' "$raw" | jq -r '.tool_name // empty' 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
[ -n "$tool" ] || exit 0

# The handoff table, written as data. Matched on the whole tool name, never a
# prefix: `ExitPlanModeSomething` is not this tool, and the guard roster's
# standing lesson (CLOUD-290, CLOUD-181) is that a token which merely starts like
# the thing is not the thing.
case "$tool" in
ExitPlanMode | AskUserQuestion) ;;
*) exit 0 ;;
esac

check="$(dirname -- "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/plan-hold-check"
[ -x "$check" ] || exit 0

# Exit 1 is the only verdict that denies. Exit 2 is "could not look" and allows,
# because a broken git dir must not be the reason a session cannot reach a human.
reason=$("$check" live 2>/dev/null)
status=$?
[ "$status" -eq 1 ] || exit 0
[ -n "$reason" ] || exit 0

jq -n --arg r "Refused: $reason" '{
  hookSpecificOutput: {
    hookEventName: "PreToolUse",
    permissionDecision: "deny",
    permissionDecisionReason: $r
  }
}'
